We can roll our own scrolls in the game by using magic markers
on scrolls of blank papers. It occured to me that spellbooks are
also paper products, so why not roll our own spellbooks the same
way we do scrolls? However, since there are different levels of
spells, the process of writing spellbooks ought to be more
sophisticated.

Perhaps useless spellbooks may be turned into spellbooks of
*blank papers* by dipping them into water *several* times
(or by cancelling them.) Then we can use magic markers to write
the spellbooks of our choice (only the ones that have been
discovered before.) The success of writing a spellbook of say
level n would depend on the intelligence level of the character
and the charges the magic marker he/she is going to use has.
A magic marker should have 10*n+c charges where c is a random
number between 1 and 9 for a spellbook of level n. For instance,
if a character has In:15 and 30+ charges on the magic marker,
he/she can try to write only a spellbook of level 3 or less.
If there are not enough charges on the magic marker, it becomes
useles and the spellbook of blank papers disappears. Not having
enough intelligence should have a similar effect and confuse,
blind or paralyze the character as well. Writing a spellbook
should take as much time as required to transcribe it.

This idea can be complemented with a new tool: binding kit.
Using a binding kit, several scrolls of blank papers may be
bound together to obtain a spellbook of blank papers.
Furthermore several scrolls of the same type such as light,
create monster, confuse monster or identify may be bound
together to obtain a spellbook of the same.

How does this sound?

Mehmet.


